  <body>
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="masthead">
        <p class="wordmark"><a href={base}>The Forge <span class="rule-red">/</span> Ecosystem</a></p>
        <nav aria-label="Primary">
          <a href={`${base}guides/`}>Guides</a>
          <a href="https://github.com/albetnov/forge-flutter">GitHub</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="band">
        <h1>sqlite_schema_forge <span class="t-red">quick start</span></h1>
        <p class="lede">
          A declarative SQLite schema manager with auto-migration. Declare the
          desired state in plain Dart objects and let the diffing engine write
          the transitions: no up and down files, no migration manager, no
          raw CREATE TABLE strings.
        </p>
        <div class="meta">
          <span class="ver">0.1.0</span>
          <a href="https://pub.dev/packages/sqlite_schema_forge">pub.dev</a>
          <a href="https://github.com/albetnov/forge-flutter/tree/main/packages/sqlite_schema_forge">README source</a>
        </div>
      </div>

      <ol class="toc" aria-label="Stops on this line">
        <li><a href="#install"><span class="stop">S1</span>Install the line</a></li>
        <li><a href="#declare"><span class="stop">S2</span>Declare the desired state</a></li>
        <li><a href="#migrate"><span class="stop">S3</span>Migrate, preview, control</a></li>
        <li><a href="#relate"><span class="stop">S4</span>Keys, search, triggers</a></li>
        <li><a href="#skills"><span class="stop">S5</span>Agent skills</a></li>
              </ol>

      <div class="prose">
        <section class="block" id="install" aria-labelledby="install-h">
          <h2 id="install-h">Install the line</h2>
          <p>
            One dependency. The package is driver agnostic: it runs on any
            SQLite driver (sqflite, sqlite3, FFI) through the lightweight
            <code class="inline">DatabaseExecutor</code> contract, and reads
            <code class="inline">PRAGMA user_version</code> on startup so warm
            launches cost under a millisecond.
          </p>
          <figure class="code">
            <figcaption><span>terminal</span><button class="copy" data-copy="code-install">Copy</button></figcaption>
            <pre><code id="code-install" set:html={highlight(installCmd)} /></pre>
          </figure>
        </section>

        <section class="block" id="declare" aria-labelledby="declare-h">
          <h2 id="declare-h">Declare the desired state</h2>
          <p>
            Define schemas with plain Dart objects: a versioned
            <code class="inline">Schema</code> holding <code class="inline">Table</code> entries, each a list of
            <code class="inline">Column</code> declarations plus indexes. No callback wrappers.
          </p>
          <figure class="code">
            <figcaption><span>desired state, declared</span><button class="copy" data-copy="code-declare">Copy</button></figcaption>
            <pre><code id="code-declare" set:html={highlight(declareSnippet)} /></pre>
          </figure>
          <p>Then point the engine at a live database and migrate to it:</p>
          <figure class="code">
            <figcaption><span>migrate to target</span><button class="copy" data-copy="code-migrate">Copy</button></figcaption>
            <pre><code id="code-migrate" set:html={highlight(migrateSnippet)} /></pre>
          </figure>
          <div class="note">
            <p><span class="lead">Fast-path boot:</span> the engine checks <code class="inline">PRAGMA user_version</code> first. When the live version already matches the target, migration is a sub-millisecond no-op.</p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="block" id="migrate" aria-labelledby="migrate-h">
          <h2 id="migrate-h">Migrate, preview, control</h2>
          <p>
            The diffing engine introspects live SQLite <code class="inline">PRAGMA</code> metadata and generates native <code class="inline">ALTER TABLE</code> statements or safe copy-and-swap rebuilds. Manual up and down files do not exist in this model.
          </p>
          <h3>Transform data around structure</h3>
          <p>
            When a structural change needs data work first (splitting a column, formatting before a type change), <code class="inline">onUpgrade</code> and <code class="inline">onDowngrade</code> run inside the migration. Destructive steps require opting in with <code class="inline">allowDestructive</code>.
          </p>
          <figure class="code">
            <figcaption><span>upgrade with data work</span><button class="copy" data-copy="code-upgrade">Copy</button></figcaption>
            <pre><code id="code-upgrade" set:html={highlight(upgradeSnippet)} /></pre>
          </figure>
          <h3>Preview before committing</h3>
          <p>
            <code class="inline">plan()</code> computes the same migration as a dry run: inspect every step and its SQL, and check <code class="inline">hasDestructiveChanges</code> before anything writes.
          </p>
          <figure class="code">
            <figcaption><span>dry run</span><button class="copy" data-copy="code-plan">Copy</button></figcaption>
            <pre><code id="code-plan" set:html={highlight(planSnippet)} /></pre>
          </figure>
          <h3>External version control</h3>
          <p>
            If application versioning lives outside SQLite (a multi-storage setup alongside Hive or DataStore, for example), <code class="inline">applyPlan()</code> executes a plan without touching <code class="inline">PRAGMA user_version</code>.
          </p>
          <figure class="code">
            <figcaption><span>versionless apply</span><button class="copy" data-copy="code-apply">Copy</button></figcaption>
            <pre><code id="code-apply" set:html={highlight(applySnippet)} /></pre>
          </figure>
        </section>

        <section class="block" id="relate" aria-labelledby="relate-h">
          <h2 id="relate-h">Keys, search, triggers</h2>
          <p>
            Relationships, full-text search, and triggers all live inside the same declarative <code class="inline">Schema</code>, and the diffing engine migrates them in dependency order: tables, then virtual tables, then triggers.
          </p>
          <h3>Foreign keys</h3>
          <p>
            SQLite disables foreign key enforcement on new connections, so enable it per connection. References themselves are declared on the column:
          </p>
          <figure class="code">
            <figcaption><span>reference by declaration</span><button class="copy" data-copy="code-fk">Copy</button></figcaption>
            <pre><code id="code-fk" set:html={highlight(fkSnippet)} /></pre>
          </figure>
          <h3>Full-text search tables</h3>
          <p>
            <code class="inline">VirtualTable.fts5()</code> (FTS4 and FTS3 work the same way) binds a search index directly to its content table. The engine inspects <code class="inline">sqlite_master</code> to create or rebuild virtual tables as definitions change.
          </p>
          <figure class="code">
            <figcaption><span>bound FTS index</span><button class="copy" data-copy="code-fts">Copy</button></figcaption>
            <pre><code id="code-fts" set:html={highlight(ftsSnippet)} /></pre>
          </figure>
          <h3>Triggers</h3>
          <p>
            Use the zero-boilerplate FTS sync preset, or a custom trigger object with explicit timing, event, and body:
          </p>
          <figure class="code">
            <figcaption><span>preset or custom</span><button class="copy" data-copy="code-trigger">Copy</button></figcaption>
            <pre><code id="code-trigger" set:html={highlight(triggerSnippet)} /></pre>
          </figure>
        </section>

        <section class="block" id="skills" aria-labelledby="skills-h">
          <h2 id="skills-h">Agent skills</h2>
          <p>
            <code class="inline">sqlite_schema_forge</code> also ships its
            guidance as agent skills: prescriptive rules and examples authored
            for Cursor, Claude Code, Copilot, Cline, and any other agent working
            in your repository. Each skill is a <code class="inline">SKILL.md</code>
            shipped inside the package, so the advice is versioned with the API
            you actually installed.
          </p>
          <figure class="code">
            <figcaption><span>install the package skills</span><button class="copy" data-copy="code-skills">Copy</button></figcaption>
            <pre><code id="code-skills" set:html={highlight(skillsCmd)} /></pre>
          </figure>
          <p>Two skills ship with <code class="inline">sqlite_schema_forge</code>:</p>
          <table class="scope">
            <tbody>
              <tr><th scope="row">sqlite-schema-forge-schema</th><td>Declaring Schema, Table, Column, Index, VirtualTable, Trigger, and RawSql, plus the DDL they generate.</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">sqlite-schema-forge-migration</th><td>plan vs applyPlan vs migrate: the destructive guard, user_version handling, callbacks, transactions.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
          <div class="note">
            <p><span class="lead">Where they land:</span> run the command in an app that already depends on <code class="inline">sqlite_schema_forge</code>. The installer checks each directory's package prefix and writes the matching skills to your project's <code class="inline">.agents/skills/</code>.</p>
          </div>
        </section>
      </div>

      <div class="onward">
        <a href={`${base}guides/fetch-forge`}>← fetch_forge guide</a>
        <a href={`${base}guides/sqlite-query-forge`}>Next: sqlite_query_forge guide →</a>
      </div>
    </div>

    <footer>
      <div class="wrap">
        <span>This guide derives from the sqlite_schema_forge README, which stays authoritative.</span>
        <span><a href="https://github.com/albetnov/forge-flutter">Monorepo</a> · <a href="https://github.com/albetnov/forge-flutter/blob/main/LICENSE">MIT</a> · <a href="https://pub.dev/packages/sqlite_schema_forge">pub.dev</a></span>
      </div>
    </footer>

    <script>
      document.querySelectorAll(".copy").forEach((btn) => {
        btn.addEventListener("click", async () => {
          const el = document.getElementById(btn.dataset.copy);
          const done = () => {
            btn.textContent = "Copied";
            setTimeout(() => (btn.textContent = "Copy"), 1600);
          };
          try {
            await navigator.clipboard.writeText(el.textContent);
            done();
          } catch {
            const r = document.createRange();
            r.selectNodeContents(el);
            const s = getSelection();
            s.removeAllRanges();
            s.addRange(r);
            done();
          }
        });
      });
    </script>
  </body>
</html>
